{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ולא יראו פני ריקם", :translit "v'lo yira'u fanai reikam", :gloss "and they shall not appear before Me empty-handed"} {:he "ששת ימים", :translit "sheshet yamim", :gloss "six days"} {:he "נדרים ונדבות", :translit "nadarim v'nedavot", :gloss "vows and freewill offerings"} {:he "יו\"ט", :translit "yom tov", :gloss "Yom Tov / Festival"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Smikhut parshiyot: juxtaposition of 'empty-handed' with 'six days' teaches the law"}], :frame {:text "The verse states that one must not appear before God empty-handed, immediately followed by the command to work six days and rest on the seventh. Rashi (Exodus 34:21) cites the Talmudic principle that this juxtaposition teaches that vows and freewill offerings may not be brought on Yom Tov, since they were not specifically obligated for that festival. This is rooted in the rule at Chagigah 8a-9a and Bezah 11b-12a distinguishing between obligatory festival offerings (chovah) and voluntary offerings (nedavah).", :sources ["Rashi on Exodus 34:21" "Chagigah 8b" "Bezah 12a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Smikhut parshiyot: juxtaposition of 'empty-handed' with 'six days' teaches the law", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:20:56.938849662Z", :model "glm-5.2"}